/* Ownership of handshake packets (N-209, N-210).
   _nx_secure_tls_send_handshake_record() consumes its packet on every return;
   the caller owns it only before the call.  Each leg runs one TLS handshake
   between two IP instances, forces one failure, and checks:
   - the API that drove the failing step returned the code the forced failure
     produced, and the forced failure was that leg's operation, exactly once;
   - every packet of the leg's session pools is back on its free list exactly
     once: nx_packet_pool_available back at the leg's own starting value (no
     packet stranded) and nx_packet_pool_invalid_releases unchanged (no packet
     released twice).
   Each leg gets fresh session pools of its own, never used by another leg, so
   a packet stranded by one leg cannot change the counts of a later leg.
   Failures are forced by defining a library function in this executable, so
   the library's own calls (through its PLT) reach this definition first: a
   builder returns NX_SECURE_TLS_PACKET_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL without building, the
   record layer returns NX_NOT_CONNECTED without sending, or the remote
   certificate free returns NX_INVALID_PARAMETERS.  Every other call goes to
   the real function.  Nothing is overrun or corrupted.  */

